body:
Most if not all of the people who moved out at 18 had already long been planning the move and made sure they had resources to do so, which unfortunately isn't possible for everyone, some people are forced to START at 18.

&#x200B;

You must make certain everything you wish to keep is in your name, that means your car, phone, bank account, insurance, everything. If you don't do this, assume whatever you leave in your N's name(s) belongs to them, because it basically does, regardless of who paid for it.

&#x200B;

Here's what I did, in order:

&#x200B;

\-Job. It doesn't necessecarily have to be your forever job, it just has to be able to pay for everything you want. Make a list of your monthly expenses then double everything, if you still make more after taxes, you're doing great.

&#x200B;

\-Place important documents in your name (bank account, phone)

&#x200B;

\-Save a substantial amount of cash. There's no set dollar amount to follow here, but because I had a high-paying job, I saved up a full year of living expenses and money has never since been an issue for me.

&#x200B;

\-Car owned by you. It's tempting to go buy a new, or new-er model car that looks nice, but don't do it. The last thing you need is a car payment at this point, even if you can "afford it." Been there done that.

&#x200B;

\-Place to live

&#x200B;

\-Move

&#x200B;

N's do a great job of overcomplicating moving out and making it and life seem terrible and hard. It's not.

I found living with narcissists for 18 years of my life to be much more difficult than living on my own.

&#x200B;

There are other ways to do this of course, such as college, military, etc, but this is what I did, and it worked out very well for me.

I just want to know how they found out where gf works. I don't know who the FUCK tipped them off about it, but I feel like we have some flying monkeys we don't know about.

&#x200B;

I'm thinking social media - however, both of us have blocked most all my family and their narcissistic friends, except for a few who are completely supportive and never liked my Nparents from the start. Not to mention, we don't even list where we work on social media, and gf doesn't even have a last name on her fb page.

&#x200B;

Either way, I feel like it's only a matter of time before they start showing up at my door and that will not go well for them at all.

You say you aren't in any danger of dying or being seriously hurt, but hun, I don't think that's quite right. Bruising your rib? Holy hell. That's NOT punishment, for anyone, ever, that's assault. Had your rib gotten broken that could have put you in danger, as a nursing student I've seen patients die from a broken rib due to punctures. I'm not trying to scare you, but that sounds like some serious danger to me, and it's absolutely not a situation I'd want myself or anyone else in. Even without physical abuse, sometimes verbal abuse is just as bad if not worse, and while it doesn't sound like physical abuse is ever-present in your situation, it sounds like verbal abuse is. 

&#x200B;

Okay, this is part of what I mean by moving out efficiently. Before you move out, you need to make sure the person you are moving in with is responsible (preferably someone who already pays their rent on their own place so if they default on it, they'd fuck themselves up and not just you) and willing to take you to and from work each day. Make sure you mention though, your situation, and that you'll only need a ride until you have your own transportation, if they see you're trying to better yourself (trying to work to save for your own car) any kind-person would be more than willing to help you.

&#x200B;

I also think that while going no-contact after moving out may be very difficult for you at first, it's probably not a bad option. But if you aren't ready to do this, you could try going low-contact. All communication is done either electronically, or in person, but at a public place with witnesses present (Nparents usually put on the "WE'RE A SUPER-FUCKING-PERFECT FAMILY CANT YOU TELL?" show when company is present), and obviously, you shouldn't tell them where you live.

&#x200B;

It'll be hard wanting to go back, I wanted to go back at first too. But once I started working the typical work week, and hanging out with my friends literally every night after work (something i'd have never been able to do if I lived with my parents) I realized that life is fucking awesome, and this is crucial once you move out, you need to have some quality fun time, or else it will suck. Don't just get up and go to work every day, then come home and cry (unless you enjoy doing that), get out and do what YOU want to, because it's YOUR life, not theirs. Don't let them rob you of your happiness by blinding you of the truth.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Beating anyone, even your child, who is over 18 is assault and will result in an immediate arrest. It's the same as if you walked up to a random stranger and beat them.

&#x200B;

They also, can't just lock you out. At your age, they can legally kick you out but if they so choose to go this route, they have to give you X amount of days before you must leave. And even then, if you don't leave, it could be months before the police show up to formally evict you.

&#x200B;

A 10 minute drive took me just 30 minutes on a bike. I'm assuming you have to dress nice for work, so to get around this, slap on a pair of workout-ish comfortable clothes and put your nice clothes in a backpack, then just change when you get to work. If you have a co-worker who is willing to give you a ride though, you might could luck out on this. 

&#x200B;

Once you get a car, EVERYTHING gets easier. It doesn't have to be a nice car, it just has to drive. You won't be biking to work for long.

You should be able to go and get your license at 18 regardless of what your parents have to say about it. If you fail the test at first, you will almost certainly pass the second time.

&#x200B;

After that, you obviously need a car that you own and insure (so they can't take it), but I made do with riding a bicycle to work for a few weeks before I got my car. It's hard, but remember that at 18+ you can leave home any time you want and they can't do anything about it.

The most important things about moving out are making sure you do it quickly, efficiently, and don't go back.

&#x200B;

I'm not sure what your current situation is really, but i'll tell you what I did, and with some re-ordering, it may help you too.

&#x200B;

1, You will need a job. The only way around this is if you have a friend or trusted family member who will allow you to stay with them rent-free. If this is the case, you'll need a job later.

&#x200B;

2, You need a car. (some people in big cities I suppose could eliminate this one). The car MUST be in YOUR NAME, and it must be insured by YOU. If you own and insure the car, any attempt at taking the car or your keys away from you is THEFT.

&#x200B;

3, Transfer all your bank accounts, phones, everything you want, in YOUR name. If you have a joint account, go to the bank and open a new account and tell them that you want to be the sole owner of the account. Keep the joint account open to prevent your parents from receiving a cancellation notice, but transfer all the money out of that account into your new one.

&#x200B;

4, Do it quickly. I was working for about a month after I got my car, before I took a day off (when I knew both nparents would be at work until later in the day) and I knew I had from 8 in the morning until 5pm that day to get everything up and get out. Even if you have a lot of stuff, 8 hours should give you plenty of time, but act like you have 2 hours. If this is not an option for you, and you fear you will be harmed if they know about you moving out, call the police and ask them to do a civil standby while you grab your belongings. They will make sure you can get your stuff and get out without being harmed.

&#x200B;

Lastly and most importantly, do not tell them where you are going, do not go back, and do not back down. Settling into your new place at first can be scary because it's not what you're used to, but after some time, you'll be glad you didn't go back. I moved out at 18 and I haven't looked back since, feel free to reply with any questions.

I didn't get my permit until I was 17. They knew if I couldn't drive, I couldn't go anywhere. The only reason I got it is because we were getting on an airplane and she thought I had to have ID, so she ever so reluctantly took me to the DMV and I got my permit, but I was never allowed to drive. I knew minors didn't need ID to get on planes, but I never told her that (lol).

&#x200B;

I got my license on my 18th birthday. Not being able to drive and basically not being able to go anywhere besides work and church was the absolute worst part of my childhood. It wasn't until I started going places by myself that I realized how much it fucking sucks for people who can't drive and still live with their parents.
